<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sen no Rikyū</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sen no Rikyu, Sen Rikyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sen no Rikyū was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> master from a merchant family of Sakai who shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> into its classic form. He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, for whom he organised great tea gatherings, and promoted wabi, a rustic and simple aesthetic. In 1591 he fell from Hideyoshi's favour for reasons still debated and was ordered to commit ritual suicide.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu.jpg" alt="Sen no Rikyū" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: painted by 長谷川等伯, calligraphy by 春屋宗園 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sen_no_Rikyu_JPN_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sen no Rikyū was born in Sakai, now in Osaka Prefecture. His father was a warehouse owner named Tanaka Yohei, who later also used the family name Sen. As a young man he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> under the Sakai townsman Kitamuki Dōchin, and at nineteen, through Dōchin's introduction, he began to study under Takeno Jō'ō, who is also associated with the development of the wabi aesthetic. He is believed to have received the Buddhist name Sōeki from the Rinzai Zen priest Dairin Sōtō of Nanshū-ji in Sakai. He married around the age of 21 and trained in Zen at Daitoku-ji in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Little is known about his middle years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1579, at the age of 58, Rikyū became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> master to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, and after Nobunaga's death in 1582 he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>. He entered Hideyoshi's circle of confidants and became the most influential figure in the world of chanoyu. In 1585 he needed extra credentials to enter the Imperial Palace to help at a tea gathering Hideyoshi gave for Emperor Ōgimachi, and the emperor bestowed on him the lay name and title Rikyū Koji. He was also central to the Grand Kitano Tea Ceremony of 1587.</p><p>In his later years he began to use very small rustic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> rooms called sō-an, 'grass hermitage'. The two-tatami room Tai-an at Myōki-an temple in Yamazaki, credited to his design, is a National Treasure. Raku ware tea bowls originated in his collaboration with a tile-maker named Raku Chōjirō. He preferred simple, local wares to the costly Chinese items then fashionable. Though he did not invent the philosophy of wabi-sabi, he is among those most responsible for popularising it and bringing it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a>. His style became known as sōan-cha or wabi-cha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hideyoshi ordered Rikyū to commit ritual suicide, apparently because of crucial differences of opinion and because Rikyū was too independent. One year earlier Hideyoshi had had Rikyū's disciple Yamanoue Sōji tortured and beheaded. Hideyoshi's reason may never be known for certain. Rikyū committed seppuku at his residence in Hideyoshi's Jurakudai palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> on 21 April 1591, at the age of seventy. Okakura Kakuzō wrote that his last act was to hold a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> and that he left a death poem. Three houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, the Omotesenke, Urasenke and Mushakōjisenke, descend directly from him. His grave is at the Jukō-in sub-temple in the Daitoku-ji compound.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sen no Rikyū” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The tea masters Takeno Joo and <b>Sen no Rikyu</b> came from its merchant class, and the Jesuit Gaspar Vilela described it as a republic like Venice.</blockquote><a href="/state/sakai">Sakai</a></li><li><blockquote>He expelled Jesuit missionaries in 1587 and patronised tea master <b>Sen no Rikyu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/toyotomi-regime">Toyotomi regime</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sakai, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.57,135.48&amp;year=1566" aria-label="Sakai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sen no Rikyū"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3989 4545h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3989 4545h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.57,135.48&amp;year=1566">What was here in 1566? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
